WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:05.210
Unless you've been hiding under a rock, you've
probably seen or at least heard of Bird Box.

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:10.000
The conceptual, post-apocalyptic thriller
from Netflix has taken social media by storm,

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.969
sparking internet memes, jokes, and even a
potentially dangerous social media challenge.

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:19.070
For many viewers, the most interesting aspect
of the movie involved the creatures in the

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:23.170
film, monsters that either drive people to
kill themselves or to become fanatics who

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:24.779
kill on their behalf.

00:00:24.779 --> 00:00:29.341
The mysterious creatures drive people mad
on sight, but in the finished movie, they

00:00:29.341 --> 00:00:31.109
go completely unseen.

00:00:31.109 --> 00:00:35.700
Neither Bird Box nor the book it's based on
ever show the creatures in physical form,

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.030
only adding to their mystery.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:41.960
Now, thanks to creature designer and sculptor
Andy Bergholtz and special effects studio

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:47.550
SFX Atlas, we finally know what Bird Box's
monsters would have looked like on screen,

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.530
had plans for the movie gone differently.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:53.990
Bergholtz, who is credited as the special
effects makeup lead for Bird Box, took to

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:58.210
Instagram in early January 2019 to share several
pieces of concept art.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:02.050
Together, the pictures work to reveal the
creature design that the filmmakers almost

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:03.970
used in the final movie.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:08.320
According to Bergholtz, the intention for
the movie was for no two people see the creatures

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:12.740
in the same way, with their physical forms
changing depending on who is looking at them.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:16.670
The design featured in the photos he shared
is how one of the creatures would have looked

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:18.890
to Sandra Bullock's character, Malorie.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:24.140
The images show a baby-faced monster with
a bulbous, vein-covered head, swollen ears,

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:27.370
and a snake-like body meant to be created
using CGI.

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:31.500
Reportedly, the creature is what Malorie would
have seen in a nightmarish dream sequence

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.130
that was ultimately scrapped from the final
cut.

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:38.340
As Bergholtz describes it, the custom-made
creature design was inspired by Malorie's

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:42.190
pregnancy, playing on the anxieties and fears
of an expectant mother.

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:46.060
He wrote on Instagram, "...the producers felt
Sandra's nightmare would have something to

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:49.810
do with a twisted, demonic baby creature attacking
her."

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:53.460
Bergholtz says he designed several different
takes on the monster design before settling

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.080
on a final product.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:00.600
He wrote, "I sculpted at least 3 or 4 variations
on the design before it was approved, which

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.990
began as a more aggressive, monstery look
and was revised to be a bit more subtle in

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:05.880
the end."

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:10.979
After the release of Bird Box, the company
SFX Atlas also took to Instagram to share

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:13.920
what the monster design might have looked
like in action.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.980
The company's post shows special effects artist
Stephen Prouty applying prosthetics and makeup

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:22.370
to actor Dirk Rogers, who would have stood
in for the creature during the shooting of

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:23.800
the nightmare sequence.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:27.820
Allegedly, not everyone who helped create
the designs for the monsters is happy that

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:31.590
their work ended up getting cut from the movie,
especially after the film proved to be so

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:33.260
widely-seen.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:38.620
Howard Berger, the co-owner of KNB EFX Group,
where the designs for the Bird Box creatures

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:42.930
were crafted, seemed to air his complaints
in a since-deleted Instagram post, writing,

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:46.970
"It's always a bit disappointing when so much
effort goes into something that ends up in

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:51.170
the cutting room floor, but I get it and it's
always what is best for the final product."

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:55.530
When Bird Box was released, some viewers found
it annoying that the movie never shows what

00:02:55.530 --> 00:03:00.030
the monsters actually look like, making the
unused creature effects extra interesting.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:05.060
But despite how unsettling the designs appear,
some fans have argued that the decision to

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:09.400
keep the monsters off screen was to the movie's
benefit, letting viewers come up with their

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.160
own interpretations of what lurks beyond the
blindfolds.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.010
Sandra Bullock seems to feel the same way.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:19.100
Speaking in an interview with horror movie
site Bloody Disgusting, Bullock said she couldn't

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:23.690
help but laugh at the creature in action,
which she described as, quote, "a long fat

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:24.690
baby."

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:28.330
Turns out, the choice of whether to show the
creatures or not held a lot of importance.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:32.220
If viewers found the monsters funny, it could
have ruined the whole film.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:36.319
Director Susanne Bier apparently agreed, and
pushed to remove the nightmare moment from

00:03:36.319 --> 00:03:37.319
the movie.

00:03:37.319 --> 00:03:38.940
As she put it to Bloody Disgusting,

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:40.900
"It so easily becomes funny.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:44.950
We actually shot that [scene] and spent a
lot of energy on [it], but every time I saw

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:47.780
it, I was like, 'This is not going to be tense.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.060
It's just going to be funny.'"

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:53.129
Now that the movie is out in the wild, people
seem to agree that keeping the monsters off

00:03:53.129 --> 00:03:55.260
screen was the right choice.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:59.610
As Bergholtz wrote in his Instagram post,
"I actually really liked the movie and think

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:02.019
it was better off NOT showing the makeup.

00:04:02.019 --> 00:04:05.599
Kudos to the director for sticking to her
guns on that one."

